 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [LLFF Dataset Loading](2818-llff-dataset-loading.md)
-Loads real-world LLFF dataset scenes, parsing colmap poses from `poses_bounds.npy`, correcting axis conventions, and applying NDC normalization. Unlike Blender data, LLFF data does not use a white background (`WHITE_BG=False`) and requires NDC transformation for uniform sampling.
+Loads real-world LLFF (Local Light Field Fusion) dataset scenes, parsing COLMAP poses from `poses_bounds.npy`, correcting axis conventions, and applying NDC (Normalized Device Coordinates) normalization. It differs from general NeRF dataset loading by enforcing forward-facing scene assumptions and specific axis corrections required for the LLFF capture rig.
+
+### [NeRF Dataset Loading](3084-nerf-dataset-loading.md)
+This method loads the NeRF-Synthetic (Blender) dataset by parsing JSON metadata for camera poses and images, handling half-resolution resizing, and returning normalized RGBA images and camera parameters. It differs from [LLFF Dataset Loading](2818-llff-dataset-loading.md) by assuming known, precise camera poses provided in JSON rather than estimating them from images, and by using a fixed scene bounding box.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot H \cdot W)$ for preprocessing, where $N$ is the number of images [S1, unofficial]. Ray generation is linear in the number of pixels [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot H \cdot W \cdot 6)$ to store all ray origins and directions [S1, unofficial]. This can be significant for high-resolution images [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Precomputing rays allows for fast training iterations, as ray generation is moved out of the training loop [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Handles both synthetic and real-world data formats [S1, unofficial]. Efficient batching via precomputed rays [S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** High memory usage for large datasets [S1, unofficial]. NDC normalization is only suitable for forward-facing scenes [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of pixels in the selected images for preprocessing. Sampling is constant time per batch.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of rays in the batch. Precomputed rays for all images can be large for high-resolution datasets.
+- **Strengths:** Efficient for synthetic datasets with known poses. Simple preprocessing pipeline.
+- **Limitations:** Assumes known camera poses. Not suitable for real-world datasets without pose estimation.
+- **When to use:** When training NeRF on synthetic Blender datasets.
+- **When not to use:** When training on real-world datasets with unknown poses (use [LLFF Dataset Loading](2818-llff-dataset-loading.md) or similar).
 
 ## Background
-NeRF (Neural Radiance Fields) was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1, S2, unofficial]. The data preparation methods described here are based on the official NeRF implementation and subsequent popular open-source repositories like `nerf-from-scratch` and `instant-nsr-pl` [S1, S2, unofficial]. The Blender dataset is a standard synthetic benchmark, while the LLFF dataset is a standard real-world forward-facing benchmark [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Originated with the NeRF paper [S1, S2, unofficial]. Used for training NeRF on synthetic Blender datasets [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
